TY  - JOUR
ID  - balci2022machinelearning
AU  - Balci, Mehmet
AU  - Tasdemir, Sakir
AU  - Ozmen, Guzin
AU  - Golcuk, Adem
TI  - Machine Learning-Based Detection of Sleep-Disordered Breathing Type Using Time and Time-Frequency Features
PY  - 2022
T2  - Biomedical Signal Processing and Control
JO  - Biomedical Signal Processing and Control
DO  - 10.1016/j.bspc.2021.103402
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bspc.2021.103402
PB  - Elsevier
KW  - machine learning
KW  - feature engineering
KW  - CSA / central apnea detection
KW  - PSG
KW  - SpO2 / oximetry
KW  - ECG
KW  - Snoring / sleep audio
KW  - PSG and HSAT signal analysis
KW  - Audio, oximetry, ECG, PPG, and respiratory-signal diagnosis
KW  - Elsevier
AB  - Sleep-disordered breathing is a disease that many people experience unconsciously and can have very serious consequences that can result in death. Therefore, it is extremely important to analyze the data obtained from the patient during sleep. It has become inevitable to use computer technologies in the diagnosis or treatment of many diseases in the medical field. Especially, advanced software using artificial intelligence methods in the diagnosis and decision-making processes of physicians is becoming increasingly widespread. In this study, we aimed to classify the sleep-disordered breathing type by using machine learning techniques utilizing time and time- frequency domain features. We used Pressure Flow, electrocardiogram, Pressure Snore, SpO2, Pulse and Thorax data from among the polysomnography of 19 patients. We employed digital signal processing methods for six types of physiological data and obtained a total of 35 features using different feature extraction methods for five different classes (Normal, Hypopnea, obstructive sleep apnea, Mixed Apnea, Central Apnea). Finally, we applied machine learning algorithms (ANN, Support Vector Machine, Random Forest, Naive Bayes, K Nearest Neighborhood, Decision Tree and Logistic Regression) on 5-class and 35-feature data sets. We used10 fold cross-validation to verify the classification success. Our main contribution to the literature is that we developed a classification system to score all four different types of sleep-disordered breathing simultaneously by using 6 types of polysomnography. As a five-class scoring result, the Random Forest (RF) algorithm showed the highest success with 76.3 % classification. When Hypopnea was excluded from the evaluation, classification increased to 86.6% for three Apnea-type disorders. Our proposed method provided 89.7% accuracy for the diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea by the RF classifier. The results show that time and time–frequency domain features are distinctive in Sleep-disordered breathing scoring, which is a very difficult process for physicians and a diagnostic support system can be design by evaluating many polysomnography simultaneously. Data modality: PSG pressure flow; ECG; snore; SpO2; pulse; thorax signals. Clinical task: Classify normal, hypopnea, obstructive apnea, mixed apnea, central apnea. AI method: Time/time-frequency features; ML classifiers. Main finding: Supports oximetry-based detection or severity estimation using desaturation and overnight oxygen-pattern information.. Taxonomy groups: PSG and HSAT signal analysis; Audio, oximetry, ECG, PPG, and respiratory-signal diagnosis.
N1  - Included core paper for AI/ML/DL OSA/SDB review. Source=Elsevier; Groups=G1; G3; CitationKey=balci2022machinelearning
ER  -

