TY  - JOUR
ID  - hamm2023entropysnoring
AU  - Bahr-Hamm, K.
AU  - Abriani, A.
AU  - Anwar, A.R.
AU  - Ding, H.
AU  - Muthuraman, M.
AU  - Gouveris, H.
TI  - Using entropy of snoring, respiratory effort and electrocardiography signals during sleep for OSA detection and severity classification
PY  - 2023
T2  - Sleep Medicine
JO  - Sleep Medicine
DO  - 10.1016/j.sleep.2023.09.005
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.sleep.2023.09.005
PB  - Elsevier
KW  - feature engineering
KW  - entropy features
KW  - SVM
KW  - OSA detection
KW  - Severity classification
KW  - Respiratory effort
KW  - ECG
KW  - Snoring / sleep audio
KW  - PSG and HSAT signal analysis
KW  - Audio, oximetry, ECG, PPG, and respiratory-signal diagnosis
KW  - Phenotyping, endotyping, and risk stratification
KW  - Elsevier
AB  - Study objectives: obstructive sleep apnea is a very prevalent disease and its diagnosis is based on poly- somnography. We investigated whether snoring-sound-, very low frequency electrocardiogram (electrocardiogram-VLF)- and thoraco-abdominal effort- polysomnography signal entropy values could be used as surrogate markers for detection of obstructive sleep apnea and obstructive sleep apnea classification. Methods: The raw data of the snoring-, electrocardiogram- and abdominal and thoracic excursion signal recordings of two consecutive full-night polysomnography of 86 consecutive patients (22 female, 53.74 ± 12.4 years) were analyzed retro- spectively. Four epochs (30 s each, manually scored according to the American Academy of Sleep Medicine standard) of each sleep stage (N1, N2, N3, REM, awake) were used as the ground truth. Sampling entropy (SampEn) of all the above signals was calculated and group comparisons between the obstructive sleep apnea groups were performed. In total, (86x4x5 = )1720 epochs/group/night were included in the training set as an input for a support vector machine algorithm to classify the obstructive sleep apnea classes. Analyses were performed for first- and second-night polysomnography recordings separately. Results: Twenty-seven patients had mild (RDI = ≥ 5/h but <15/h), 21 patients moderate (RDI ≥15/h but <30/h) and 23 patients obstructive sleep apnea (RDI ≥30/h). Fifteen patients had an RDI <5/h and were therefore considered non- obstructive sleep apnea. Using SE on the above three polysomnography signal data and using a support vector machine pipeline, it was possible to distinguish between the four obstructive sleep apnea classes. The best metric was snoring signal-SE. The area-under-the-curve (AUC) calculations showed reproducible significant results for both nights of polysomnography. The second night data were even more significant, with non-obstructive sleep apnea (R) vs. light obstructive sleep apnea (L) 0.61, R vs. moderate (M) 0.68, R vs. heavy obstructive sleep apnea (H) 0.84, L vs. M 0.63, M vs. H 0.65 and L vs. H 0.82. The results were not confounded by age or gender. Conclusions: SampEn of either snoring-, very low electrocardiogram-frequencies- or thoraco-abdominal effort signals alone may be used as a surrogate marker to diagnose obstructive sleep apnea and even predict obstructive sleep apnea. More specifically, in this exploratory study snoring signal SampEn showed the greatest predictive accuracy for obstructive sleep apnea among the three signals. Second night data showed even more accurate results for all three parameters than first-night recordings. Therefore, technologies using only parts of the polysomnography signal, e.g. sound-recording devices, may be used for obstructive sleep apnea screening and obstructive sleep apnea group classification. Data modality: Snoring; respiratory effort; ECG-VLF. Clinical task: OSA detection and severity classification. AI method: Entropy features; SVM. Main finding: Demonstrates audio-based or speech-based screening for OSA/SDB, supporting low-burden contactless assessment.. Taxonomy groups: PSG and HSAT signal analysis; Audio, oximetry, ECG, PPG, and respiratory-signal diagnosis; Phenotyping, endotyping, and risk stratification.
N1  - Included core paper for AI/ML/DL OSA/SDB review. Source=Elsevier; Groups=G1; G3; G5; CitationKey=hamm2023entropysnoring
