TI  - Application and interpretation of machine learning models in predicting the risk of severe obstructive sleep apnea in adults
PY  - 2023
T2  - BMC Medical Informatics and Decision Making
JO  - BMC Medical Informatics and Decision Making
DO  - 10.1186/s12911-023-02331-z
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1186/s12911-023-02331-z
PB  - Springer Nature
KW  - machine learning
KW  - attention mechanism
KW  - gradient boosting
KW  - logistic regression
KW  - explainable AI
KW  - Severity classification
KW  - Risk prediction
KW  - Explainability / trustworthiness
KW  - Clinical variables
KW  - Phenotyping, endotyping, and risk stratification
KW  - Explainable, trustworthy, and clinically deployable AI
KW  - SpringerLink
AB  - Background obstructive sleep apnea is a globally prevalent disease with a complex diagnostic method. obstructive sleep apnea is associated with multi-system dysfunction. We aimed to develop an interpretable machine learning (ML) model for predicting the risk of obstructive sleep apnea and analyzing the risk based on clinical characteristics and questionnaires. Methods This was a retrospective study comprising 1656 subjects who presented and underwent polysomnography between 2018 and 2021. A total of 23 variables were included, and after univariate analysis, 15 variables were selected for further preprocessing. Six types of classification models were used to evaluate the ability to predict obstructive sleep apnea, namely logistic regression (LR), gradient boosting machine (GBM), extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost), adaptive boosting (AdaBoost), bootstrapped aggregating (Bagging), and multilayer perceptron (MLP). All models used the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC) was calculated as the performance metric. We also drew SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) plots to interpret predictive results and to analyze the relative importance of risk. An online calculator was developed to estimate the risk of obstructive sleep apnea in individuals. Results Among the enrolled subjects, 61.47% (1018/1656) were diagnosed with obstructive sleep apnea. Multivariate LR analysis showed that 10 of 23 variables were independent risk for obstructive sleep apnea. The GBM model showed the performance (AUC = 0.857, accuracy = 0.766, sensitivity = 0.798, specificity = 0.734). An online calculator was developed to estimate the risk of obstructive sleep apnea based on the GBM model. Finally, waist circumference, neck circumference, the Epworth Sleepiness Scale, age, and the Berlin questionnaire were revealed by the SHAP plot as the top five critical variables contributing to the diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea. Additionally, two typical cases were analyzed to interpret the contribution of each variable to the outcome prediction in a single patient. Conclusions We established six risk prediction models for obstructive sleep apnea using ML algorithms. Among them, the GBM model performed best. The model facilitates individualized assessment and further clinical strategies for patients with suspected obstructive sleep apnea. This will help to identify patients with obstructive sleep apnea as early as possible and ensure their timely treatment. Data modality: Clinical characteristics; questionnaires. Clinical task: Severe OSA risk prediction. AI method: LR, GBM, XGBoost, AdaBoost, bagging, MLP; SHAP. Main finding: Combines clinical variables and interpretable ML to build a severe-OSA risk calculator.. Taxonomy groups: Phenotyping, endotyping, and risk stratification; Explainable, trustworthy, and clinically deployable AI.
N1  - Included core paper for AI/ML/DL OSA/SDB review. Source=SpringerLink; Groups=G5; G7; CitationKey=shi2023application
