TY  - JOUR
ID  - parbat2024multiscaleentropy
AU  - Parbat, Debanjan
AU  - Chakraborty, Monisha
TI  - Multiscale entropy analysis of single lead ECG and ECG derived respiration for AI based prediction of sleep apnea events
PY  - 2024
T2  - Biomedical Signal Processing and Control
JO  - Biomedical Signal Processing and Control
DO  - 10.1016/j.bspc.2023.105444
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bspc.2023.105444
PB  - Elsevier
KW  - machine learning
KW  - entropy features
KW  - OSA/SDB assessment
KW  - ECG
KW  - Audio, oximetry, ECG, PPG, and respiratory-signal diagnosis
KW  - Elsevier
AB  - Sleep apnea (SA) is a prevalent sleep disorder that affects a significant portion of the adult population. The proposed method involves coarse graining a signal at different scales, using the popular multiscale entropy algorithms to detect apnea and normal events from single lead Electrocardiography (electrocardiogram), Instantaneous Heart Rate and electrocardiogram derived respiration. The idea of nonlinear dynamical systems analysis for feature extraction along with machine learning, the authors present here a moderately accurate sleep apnea detection model with an accuracy ranging from 70% to 100% based on two different probabilistic thresholds of 50 % and 70% with reduction in false positive rate from 28% to 14 % for applications in development of artificial intelligence based IoT connected smart wearable devices. The two thresholding modes offer a choice to do a trade-off between high training accuracy with high false positive rate and low accuracy with low false positive rate. The performance metrics of each model have been reported and choice is made for best suitable model with prospectives of implementation for diagnostic purposes. The effectiveness of the proposed method in accurate detection of sleep apnea requires further validation of the models with out of distribution dataset. Data modality: Single-lead ECG; instantaneous HR; ECG-derived respiration. Clinical task: Sleep-apnea event prediction/classification. AI method: Multiscale entropy; ML. Main finding: Shows that ECG/HRV-derived patterns can detect sleep-apnea events or classify OSA severity using reduced-channel data.. Taxonomy groups: Audio, oximetry, ECG, PPG, and respiratory-signal diagnosis.
N1  - Included core paper for AI/ML/DL OSA/SDB review. Source=Elsevier; Groups=G3; CitationKey=parbat2024multiscaleentropy
ER  -

