TY  - JOUR
ID  - eris2025huge
AU  - Bulut Eris, Seval
AU  - Recep Bozkurt, Mehmet
AU  - Eris, Omer
AU  - Bilgin, Cahit
TI  - A Huge Innovation in Diagnosis of Obstructive Sleep Apnea Syndrome: With an Artificial Intelligence-Based Algorithm, Obstructive Sleep Apnea Syndrome Can Now Be Diagnosed With Pulmonary Function Test
PY  - 2025
T2  - IEEE Access
JO  - IEEE Access
DO  - 10.1109/ACCESS.2025.3531501
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2025.3531501
PB  - IEEE
KW  - feature selection
KW  - Diagnosis
KW  - Pulmonary function test
KW  - Audio, oximetry, ECG, PPG, and respiratory-signal diagnosis
KW  - Phenotyping, endotyping, and risk stratification
KW  - Explainable, trustworthy, and clinically deployable AI
KW  - IEEE Xplore
AB  - obstructive sleep apnea is a life-threatening disease characterized by upper airway narrowing or obstruction. The diagnosis is difficult, costly, and time-consuming. Many individuals with obstructive sleep apnea do not apply for a diagnosis or are unaware of their disease. This study aimed to develop a practical, fast, and reliable diagnostic system for early diagnosis and treatment of obstructive sleep apnea. For the first time, features were extracted from flow-volume curves obtained using a Pulmonary Function Test (PFT), and an Artificial Intelligence (artificial intelligence)-based algorithm was developed to diagnose obstructive sleep apnea. Spearman correlation coefficients determined the degree of influence of the features in determining obstructive sleep apnea. Several models were created using different features and artificial intelligence according to their effect levels. The models obtained by hyperparameter optimization and cross-validation were tested with unseen data, and their performance was evaluated using seven different criteria. Using only five features extracted from the flow-volume curve (TLC/PIF, PIF/PEF, TLC/FIF50, TLC/FIF25, and FIF25/FEF25), obstructive sleep apnea was diagnosed with 97.1% accuracy using the Neural Network (NN) algorithm. The results showed that obstructive sleep apnea can be diagnosed quickly and reliably using PFT available at every hospital. The features extracted from the flow-volume curve could be used as biomarkers for diagnosing obstructive sleep apnea. The proposed method can be adapted to PC-based spirometry devices without additional hardware developments. This is a significant innovation in both literature and practice. This method will enable early diagnosis for patients and many people unaware of their disease. This will shed light on several future studies. Data modality: Pulmonary-function-test flow-volume curves. Clinical task: OSAS diagnosis. AI method: AI classifiers and PFT feature selection. Main finding: PFT flow-volume AI classifier for OSAS diagnosis.. Taxonomy groups: Audio, oximetry, ECG, PPG, and respiratory-signal diagnosis; Phenotyping, endotyping, and risk stratification; Explainable, trustworthy, and clinically deployable AI.
N1  - Included core paper for AI/ML/DL OSA/SDB review. Source=IEEE Xplore; Groups=G3; G5; G7; CitationKey=eris2025huge
