TY  - JOUR
ID  - fang2025assessmentsimulate
AU  - Fang, Lucheng
AU  - Cai, Jie
AU  - Huang, Zilin
AU  - Tuohuti, Aikebaier
AU  - Chen, Xiong
TI  - Assessment of simulated snoring sounds with artificial intelligence for the diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea
PY  - 2025
T2  - Sleep Medicine
JO  - Sleep Medicine
DO  - 10.1016/j.sleep.2024.11.018
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.sleep.2024.11.018
PB  - Elsevier
KW  - deep learning
KW  - machine learning
KW  - Diagnosis
KW  - AHI estimation
KW  - Snoring / sleep audio
KW  - Audio, oximetry, ECG, PPG, and respiratory-signal diagnosis
KW  - Elsevier
AB  - Background Performing simulated snoring (SS) is a commonly used method to evaluate the source of snoring in obstructive sleep apnea. SS sounds is considered as a potential biomarker for obstructive sleep apnea. SS sounds can be easily recorded, which is a cost-effective method for prescreening purposes. Objective This study aimed to validate the performance of artificial intelligence (artificial intelligence) models using SS sounds for obstructive sleep apnea diagnosis. Methods All participants underwent full-night polysomnography monitoring at the sleep center. SS sounds of the participants were recorded during the laryngoscopy procedure. The audio data were processed via Python, and relevant features were extracted. obstructive sleep apnea diagnostic models were developed using three machine learning (ML) algorithms and one deep learning (DL) algorithm. The diagnostic performance was evaluated by multiple indicators. Results A total of 465 participants were included. For the support vector machine algorithm, the accuracy values at apnea-hypopnea index (AHI) levels of 5, 15, and 30 per hour were 0.914, 0.887, and 0.807, respectively. For the K-nearest neighbor algorithm, the accuracy values at AHI levels of 5, 15, and 30 per hour were 0.896, 0.872, and 0.756, respectively. For the random forest algorithm, the accuracy values at AHI levels of 5, 15, and 30 per hour were 0.905, 0.881, and 0.804, respectively. For the audio spectrogram transformer algorithm, the accuracy values at AHI levels of 5, 15, and 30 per hour were 0.926, 0.887, and 0.830, respectively. Conclusions Our study demonstrates that DL models can effectively screen and identify obstructive sleep apnea with commendable performance. In addition, the identification ability of the DL models was better than that of any of the ML models. Data modality: Simulated snoring sounds. Clinical task: OSA diagnosis at AHI thresholds. AI method: ML/DL classifiers. Main finding: Demonstrates audio-based or speech-based screening for OSA/SDB, supporting low-burden contactless assessment.. Taxonomy groups: Audio, oximetry, ECG, PPG, and respiratory-signal diagnosis.
N1  - Included core paper for AI/ML/DL OSA/SDB review. Source=Elsevier; Groups=G3; CitationKey=fang2025assessmentsimulate
