TI  - SleepECG-Net: Explainable Deep Learning Approach With ECG for Pediatric Sleep Apnea Diagnosis
PY  - 2025
T2  - IEEE Journal of Biomedical and Health Informatics
JO  - IEEE Journal of Biomedical and Health Informatics
DO  - 10.1109/JBHI.2024.3495975
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1109/JBHI.2024.3495975
PB  - IEEE
KW  - deep learning
KW  - CNN
KW  - RNN
KW  - attention mechanism
KW  - explainable AI
KW  - Diagnosis
KW  - Severity classification
KW  - Pediatric OSA assessment
KW  - Explainability / trustworthiness
KW  - ECG
KW  - Audio, oximetry, ECG, PPG, and respiratory-signal diagnosis
KW  - Explainable, trustworthy, and clinically deployable AI
KW  - IEEE Xplore
AB  - obstructive sleep apnea in children is a prevalent and serious respiratory condition linked to cardiovascular morbidity. Polysomnography, the standard diagnostic approach, faces challenges in accessibility and complexity, leading to underdiagnosis. To simplify obstructive sleep apnea diagnosis, deep learning (DL) algorithms have been developed using cardiac signals, but they often lack interpretability. Our study introduces a novel interpretable DL approach (Sleepelectrocardiogram-Net) for directly estimating obstructive sleep apnea in at-risk children. A combination of convolutional and recurrent neural networks (CNN-RNN) was trained on overnight electrocardiogram (electrocardiogram) signals. Gradient-weighted Class Activation Mapping (Grad-CAM), an explainable ai, was applied to explain model decisions and extract electrocardiogram relevant to pediatric obstructive sleep apnea. Accordingly, electrocardiogram signals from the semi-public Childhood Adenotonsillectomy Trial (CHAT, n = 1610) and Cleveland Family Study (CFS,n = 64), and the private University of Chicago (UofC, n = 981) databases were used. obstructive sleep apnea diagnostic performance reached 4-class Cohen's Kappa of 0.410, 0.335, and 0.249 in CHAT, UofC, and CFS, respectively. The propobstructive sleep apneal demonstrated improved performance with increased severity along with heightened cardiovascular risk. explainable AI findings highlighted the detection of established electrocardiogram features linked to obstructive sleep apnea, such as bradycardia-tachycardia events and delayed electrocardiogram during apnea/hypopnea occurrences, focusing on clusters of events. Furthermore, Grad-CAM heatmaps identified potential electrocardiogram indicating cardiovascular risk, such as P, T, and U waves, QT intervals, and QRS complex variations. Hence, Sleepelectrocardiogram-Net approach may improve pediatric obstructive sleep apnea diagnosis by also offering cardiac risk information, thereby increasing clinician confidence in automated systems, and promoting their effective adoption in clinical practice. Data modality: Pediatric overnight ECG. Clinical task: Pediatric OSA diagnosis and severity estimation. AI method: Explainable CNN–RNN / Grad-CAM. Main finding: Pediatric ECG/XAI model for pediatric OSA diagnosis/severity estimation.. Taxonomy groups: Audio, oximetry, ECG, PPG, and respiratory-signal diagnosis; Explainable, trustworthy, and clinically deployable AI.
N1  - Included core paper for AI/ML/DL OSA/SDB review. Source=IEEE Xplore; Groups=G3; G7; CitationKey=garciavicente2025sleepecgnet
